  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1247 PM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nThe world of Panhandle weather could get a little more interesting \nlooking forward, beginning around midweek and potentially lasting \ninto next week. Models are coming together to predict a potentially \nactive pattern during this time frame, now that there are signs the \nsubtropical jet is finally shifting further south. More \nprecipitation opportunities and slightly cooler temperatures could\nbe in the cards as a result, but are no guarantee. This doesn't \nnecessarily mean it will feel like fall with drought busting \nrainfall, but it does suggest that 100 degree heat could become \nless common with relatively more frequent rain chances. \n\nRain potential looks to peak when a weak front reaches the region \nthis Wednesday, likely set to stall somewhere over the north and \ncentral Panhandles. Improved deep layer moisture behind this front \nin conjunction with better lift from the upper level pattern favors \nscattered shower/storm development along and north of the boundary \nWed afternoon-evening (30-60% POPs). There is still great doubt \nregarding where this front stalls, but most model data agrees it \nshould be along/north of the I-40 corridor. This front then \nprogresses further south on Thursday, with higher chances to settle \nsouth of the I-40 corridor (20-40% POPs north of the boundary). We \nmay have to keep an eye on severe storm potential on Thursday, \nespecially if better CAPE & Shear parameters materialize closer \nto the boundary like the NAM and ECMWF are showing. PWATs should \nbe quite rich for this time of year (1.25-1.75\"), so even though \nprobabilities to exceed 0.5\" of rain are only 10-40%, localized \ntotals >1\" are certainly plausible from these 2 rounds of rain. \n\nCold air advection will be on the weak side with this front, but\nshould keep highs mostly below 100 on Thursday. After a late\nweek warm up, we very well may have another (more substantial?) cold\nfront to look forward too this weekend into early next week. Long\nrange guidance is still in fairly good agreement regarding the \nsynoptic pattern by then, lending moderate confidence in a more \nsignificant cool down with additional rain chances heading into \nmid-September.\n\nHarrel",
